# Type arms over a trait value: `.Square(q)` tests the type
# the trait value holds and binds that value in place; the arm names a type
# as type position names it, and the table has an `else`. arms.jet: declared
# types and a builtin implementer (`impl Int.Shape`), an arm with no binding,
# a statement table and a value table. qualified.jet: another module's type
# through its module (`.geo.Point(p)`) beside this module's own `Point`.
# generic.jet: an arm that spells a generic's arguments (`.Box<Int>(b)`), and
# a generic owner's implementation (`impl Shape` in `Box<T>`) dispatched, as
# an arm's `else` and over a list of trait values, for each instance the
# values hold. instances.jet: `.Box<Int>(b)` and `.Box<String>(b)` separate
# two instances of one generic, and a third instance reaches the `else`.
fixture: TraitValueTypeArms.fixture
step arms: jet run arms.jet
exit: 0
stdout: = "square 3\na rect\nlabel hello\nshape 12\nint 7\n4\n0\n"
step arms_o0: jet run -O0 arms.jet
exit: 0
stdout: == arms
step arms_o2: jet run -O2 arms.jet
exit: 0
stdout: == arms
step qualified: jet run qualified.jet
exit: 0
stdout: = "geo point 7\nother line\n1\n"
step qualified_o2: jet run -O2 qualified.jet
exit: 0
stdout: == qualified
step generic: jet run generic.jet
exit: 0
stdout: = "4\n1\n2\n6\n"
step generic_o0: jet run -O0 generic.jet
exit: 0
stdout: == generic
step generic_o2: jet run -O2 generic.jet
exit: 0
stdout: == generic
step instances: jet run instances.jet
exit: 0
stdout: = "int box 3\nstring box x\nanother box\n"
step instances_o0: jet run -O0 instances.jet
exit: 0
stdout: == instances
step instances_o2: jet run -O2 instances.jet
exit: 0
stdout: == instances
